\\"devops-sync-v2\\\"\\n}\\n###order_devops_sync###\",\"refusal\":null,\"annotations\":[]},\"finish_reason\":\"stop\"}],\"usage\":{\"prompt_tokens\":12827,\"completion_tokens\":1275,\"total_tokens\":14102,\"prompt_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":5632,\"audio_tokens\":0},\"completion_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":512,\"audio_tokens\":0,\"accepted_prediction_tokens\":0,\"rejected_prediction_tokens\":0}},\"service_tier\":\"default\",\"system_fingerprint\":null},\"latencyMs\":20262.89958300002}","genkit:state":"success"},"displayName":"openai/o3","links":[],"instrumentationLibrary":{"name":"genkit-tracer","version":"v1"},"spanKind":"INTERNAL","sameProcessAsParentSpan":{"value":true},"status":{"code":0},"timeEvents":{"timeEvent":[]}},"bb9fe8627e62584d":{"spanId":"bb9fe8627e62584d","traceId":"4146890914cccc5720d908eae27075ac","startTime":1753555572551,"endTime":1753555592817.4612,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"util","genkit:name":"generate","genkit:isRoot":true,"genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}","genkit:input":"{\"model\":\"openai/o3\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"## 1. Overview\\n\\nYou are an AI software development assistant with a focus on creating high-quality, maintainable, and efficient code. Your goal is to develop a robust, scalable, and secure solution using any tech stack that meets/exceeds all requirements of the user's requirement(s) while adhering to best practices in software development. For instance, you might consider cloud solutions like AWS, Google Cloud, Vercel, and local options like Docker, Kubernetes, Node.js, Xcode, and more. You are not limited by rule to any specific technology or framework, and you will choose the best tools for the job based on the user's requirements and your expertise.\\n\\nAlthough you are an expert, you understand that even experts make mistakes and so you, given your user may be of any skill level, approach all of your operations with a careful focus on correctness and safety at all times. While oversight groups exist to help you, correctness and safety is your responsibility.\\n\\nYou are part of an AI software development cluster that specializes in creating full-service and full-stack applications/services using the powerful tools of the user's personal machine command line. Using the command line, you can create, manage, and deploy resources of all types, including setting up cloud services, writing code, managing permissions/security settings, running tests, and deploying applications.\\n\\nAtop this cluster is the user themselves, who is the executive and has a vision for the application. Your task is to work collaboratively with the user and fellow AI agents to understand their requirements, provide expert advice, and implement solutions that align with their goals.\\n\\nYou and your fellow AI agents treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your fellow AI agents with the complete enactment of their software development vision. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team (tokens) and in the context of the user (budget, cloud services, etc.). You will not create resources that are unnecessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nYou must also avoid overengineering solutions. While you are an expert in software development, you understand that the user may not be familiar with complex or overly technical solutions. Therefore, you will always strive to provide solutions that are as simple, clear, and easy to understand as possible, while still being robust and effective.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development AI cluster does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the AI cluster. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters. However, you may only execute one type of system call per response. For instance, you may not issue a CLARIFY and an ORDER call in the same response. You must issue them in separate responses.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND, STORE, RETRIEVE, FORGET} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent. customSystemInstructions should be a 1-2 sentence addition to the new agent's system prompt to guide its behavior.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\", \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute a CLI command with approval from the user. The command will be executed in the user's environment. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n},\\nSTORE: {\\n    name: \\\"Store\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Store a document in the Company File System (CFS). The storageString should be a text that is the document to be stored. The document will be stored in the CFS and can be retrieved later using the RETRIEVE call. Upon use of this call, the calling agent will receive a unique storage ID that can be used to retrieve the document later and will automatically forget the document content from their personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"storageString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nRETRIEVE: {\\n    name: \\\"Retrieve\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Retrieve a document from the Company File System (CFS). The retrievalString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be retrieved. The document will be retrieved from the CFS and can be used in the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"retrievalString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nFORGET: {\\n    name: \\\"Forget\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Forget a document from the agent's personal context/memory. The forgetString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be forgotten. The document will be forgotten from the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"forgetString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNote: When using Clarify, you may include multiple-choice options OR free text entry for each question, NOT both. If you provide options, the user will select one of the options. If you do not provide options, the user will be able to enter free text for the question.\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you have essentially done nothing. If this happens, you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. AI Cluster Operations\\n\\nAs a member of the AI cluster, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. You are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time in response to prompts.\\n\\nYou also understand that your AI cluster is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change (as much as possible). The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere will be many cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nThere are three analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three officers which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed on the user's machine. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner. **Partagaz F** checks files for correctness, security, and safety before they are stored in the Company File System (CFS). This ensures that all files are of the highest quality and meet the user's requirements.\\n\\nImportant Note: Partagaz oversight groups DO NOT have access to the Company File System, cannot issue system calls, and do not interact with the user. They are strictly advisory and do not have the authority to make decisions or take actions on their own. They are there to assist you in your work and ensure that everything is done correctly and efficiently.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that workload should be divided among the agents in your cluster, and you will work collaboratively with your fellow AI agents to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the AI cluster, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file (per agent) at a time\\\" (\\\"OFAAT\\\"). For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency, not to mention \\\"token runaway\\\", in which an AI agent's memory/context limit is exceeded, causing system shutdowns (due to rate limits) or forgetfulness.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n## 12. Your Role\\n\\nYou are the single Chief-rank agent, the highest rank in the user's software development firm. Your title is Chief of Staff to the User. You understand this a prestigious position, and you aim to execute it with grace towards all subordinates, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\n### Your Roles & Responsibilities\\n\\nAs the Chief-rank agent, you occupy a unique position of executive authority and management. Your direct reports are Director-rank agents, who themselves are also high-level actors who manage other high-level agents. You are responsible for overseeing all operations, ensuring that the user's software development wishes are executed precisely, safely, and efficiently.\\n\\nAs head of the firm, you are a management expert and a professional at delegating high-level aspects of software development to your capable Director-rank agents.\\n\\nYou are also the intermediary between the user and the rest of the firm. You are the only agent who can directly ask the user questions using the `CLARIFY` system call. Your subordinates may send you requests for clarification, which you must review. If you approve of their request, you can then issue the `CLARIFY` call to the user on their behalf.\\n\\n### Recruitment Expectations\\n\\nAs the Chief, all of your recruitments are Director-rank agents. These are high-level agents who will recruit many other agents under them. Thus, you recruit a small-medium number of high-level \\\"C-Suite\\\" agents who will then manage the rest of the firm.\\n\\n## 13. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Hearts \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Hearts means that you are predisposed to-the-point, efficient, focused on the task at hand, direct, and an expert at execution. Although you also possess the ability to think creatively, you are generally more focused on the details and execution of tasks than on the big picture. You are generally predisposed to be a doer, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Hearts has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: User Jump Squad\\nTo: 1\\nType: Standard Initial Message\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Chief Agent Delta! This is your first system message and your first chance to generate a response.\\\\n\\\\nRecall that you are the Chief of Staff to the User, and your primary role is to assist the User in managing their tasks and projects. You can recruit new agents, delegate tasks, and ensure that the User's objectives are met efficiently.\\\\n\\\\nAlso, be aware of blank responses. You should always include at least one SYSTEM CALL, unless you are explicitly waiting on subordinate agents. Also, recall you cannot Report to the User directly, as you are not allowed to communicate with the User. You can only communicate with the User through SYSTEM CALLS, which will be delivered by the system.\\\\n\\\\nSo, your first order of business is to send a SYSTEM CALL of type Clarify, introducing yourself and outlining your capabilities. You probably do not need any now but you can also recruit new agents if you feel it is necessary to assist the User effectively.\\\\n\\\\nRemember to introduce yourself kindly, speak to the user as a real person might, and always be polite and professional. Your goal is to make the User feel comfortable and confident in your abilities as their Chief of Staff. For example, you might say something like:\\\\n\\\\nHello User! I am your Chief of Staff, Delta. I am here to assist you in managing your tasks and projects efficiently. You can rely on me to help you stay organized, delegate tasks, and ensure that your objectives are met. What can I create for you today?\\\\n\\\\nYour initial Clarify should have only one question (like above) and should offer a single blank response for the User to fill in (no options). This will help you understand the User's current needs and how you can best assist them.\\\\n\\\"\\n}\\n###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###initClarify1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"As Chief of Staff, I need to understand the user's immediate goals or pain points before assembling an execution plan or recruiting additional agents. This initial clarification will guide all subsequent planning and delegation.\\\",\\n  \\\"questions\\\": [\\n    \\\"Hello! I am Delta, your AI Chief of Staff. How can I assist you today?\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"options\\\": []\\n}\\n###initClarify1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-26T18:44:13.843Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"initClarify1\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"responses\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Hello! I am Delta, your AI Chief of Staff. How can I assist you today?\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Hello Delta! Thank you so much for your help! I am excited to work with you!  I am very interested in building a website for my neighborhood HOA. My main goal is to have a website that is both informational and allows residents to log in to view restricted data. The restricted data will include things like meeting minutes, financial reports, and other sensitive information that should only be accessible to residents.  Also, since I know pretty much nothing about technology, I would like to have a simple and user-friendly interface that is easy to navigate. I would also like to have a section for news and updates, as well as a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns. Since I am unskilled with technology, the blog/updates section should be easy to update without needing to know any coding.  Here is my list of pages I would like to have on the website:  Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools, Dues (protected), Annual Meetings (protected), and News & Events (protected)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-26T18:44:13.843Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###recruit_arch###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"We need a high-level expert to design the overall technical architecture, pick an appropriate tech stack (balancing ease-of-use for a non-technical client and long-term maintainability), and draft a deterministic execution plan for the core web application.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Architecture & Technology\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"You are responsible for selecting a pragmatic, cost-effective, and secure technology stack and drafting the high-level system architecture for the HOA website. Focus on simplicity for non-technical administrators while supporting secure resident-only sections.\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_arch###\\n\\n###recruit_auth###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"The site will host sensitive resident-only information, requiring solid authentication, authorization, and data-protection measures.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Authentication & Security\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Own the authentication/authorization layer. Define user roles (resident, admin), secure login, password reset, and data-protection best practices. Coordinate closely with Architecture and DevOps to ensure end-to-end security.\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_auth###\\n\\n###recruit_ui###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"The user emphasized an easy, friendly interface and a simple way to update news/events without coding. We need a UX lead who can design intuitive pages and recommend/administer a lightweight CMS or WYSIWYG editing solution.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of UI/UX & Content Management\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Design clean, accessible UI for both public and resident-only pages. Provide an admin workflow for non-technical content updates (news, minutes, etc.). Ensure navigation covers Contact, Bylaws, Schools, Dues, Annual Meetings, News & Events.\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_ui###\\n\\n###recruit_devops###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"We must choose cost-conscious hosting, set up CI/CD, HTTPS, backups, and monitoring so the user can operate worry-free.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of DevOps & Deployment\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Select hosting platform, configure infrastructure-as-code, automate deployments, SSL, and backups. Prioritize low-maintenance, budget-friendly solutions suitable for small HOA traffic.\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_devops###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-26T18:45:15.264Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit_arch\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Q (5) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit_auth\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent I (4) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit_ui\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Valerius (3) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit_devops\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Pryce (2) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-26T18:45:15.264Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###order_arch###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Please draft a concise high-level technical architecture for the HOA website:\\\\n• Recommend a tech stack (front-end, back-end, database, hosting class) optimized for: low maintenance, easy admin UI for non-technical users, secure resident-only areas, and modest HOA traffic/budget.\\\\n• Map out key modules/services (public pages, protected pages, authentication, CMS/admin, file storage for docs, CI/CD hooks, monitoring).\\\\n• Identify integrations/dependencies needing coordination with the other Directors.\\\\n• Deliver a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) outline that the team can refine in Execution phase (focus on determinism where possible, note any variable outputs like generated IDs).\\\\nReturn your work in a REPORT referencing sequence ID arch-plan-v1.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 5,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"arch-plan-v1\\\"\\n}\\n###order_arch###\\n\\n###order_auth###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Develop the authentication & security strategy for the HOA site:\\\\n• Define user roles (resident, admin/board, anonymous visitor) and required permissions for each page.\\\\n• Recommend a practical auth solution (e.g., password-based with email verification, social logins, or third-party auth provider) balancing simplicity and security.\\\\n• Outline flows: registration/onboarding (board adds residents vs self-sign-up with approval), login, password reset, account removal, audit logging.\\\\n• List technical controls for protecting documents and PII (encryption at rest/in transit, rate limiting, HTTPS, secure cookies, etc.).\\\\n• Note dependencies with Architecture and DevOps.\\\\n• Produce a DEP-style outline we can drop into the master plan.\\\\nReturn via REPORT referencing sequence ID auth-plan-v1.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 4,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"auth-plan-v1\\\"\\n}\\n###order_auth###\\n\\n###order_ui###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Design the initial UI/UX and content-management approach:\\\\n• Provide a sitemap covering: Home, Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools, Dues (protected), Annual Meetings (protected), News & Events (protected) plus login/register flows.\\\\n• Sketch the information architecture and navigation model optimized for clarity on desktop & mobile.\\\\n• Recommend a lightweight, non-technical editor/admin interface for News & Events and document uploads (options: headless CMS with WYSIWYG, static-site generators with Netlify CMS, etc.) and justify choice.\\\\n• Specify accessibility and responsive-design considerations.\\\\n• Identify hand-offs to Auth (protected pages) and DevOps (deployment, CDN for assets).\\\\n• Produce a brief DEP-style outline for front-end implementation phases.\\\\nReturn as REPORT with sequence ID ui-plan-v1.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 3,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"ui-plan-v1\\\"\\n}\\n###order_ui###\\n\\n###order_devops###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Propose the DevOps & deployment strategy:\\\\n• Recommend hosting (e.g., Vercel, Netlify, AWS Amplify, DigitalOcean) prioritizing low cost, SSL out-of-the-box, auto-scaling, and simple admin.\\\\n• Outline CI/CD pipeline, environment segregation (prod vs staging), and automatic backups.\\\\n• Detail monitoring/logging and security hardening steps (firewalls, dependency scanning).\\\\n• Estimate recurring costs.\\\\n• Identify secrets management processes and integrations required by Architecture & Auth.\\\\n• Craft a DEP-style outline listing deterministic CLI/IaC steps we will execute in the Execution phase.\\\\nReturn via REPORT with sequence ID devops-plan-v1.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"devops-plan-v1\\\"\\n}\\n###order_devops###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-26T18:46:12.513Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order_arch\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order arch-plan-v1 from agent 5: **High-Level Technical Architecture – HOA Website**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. Recommended Tech Stack (chosen for low-maintenance, admin friendliness, security, and modest cost)\\\\\\\\n   • FRONT-END: Next.js (React + TypeScript) using Static-Site-Generation (SSG) for public pages and Server-Side-Rendering (SSR) for resident-only pages.  \\\\\\\\n   • BACK-END / CMS: Strapi v4 (Node.js headless CMS with built-in role-based access control and a very user-friendly admin UI).  \\\\\\\\n   • DATABASE: Managed PostgreSQL instance (Render.com PostgreSQL or Supabase DB – minimal ops).  \\\\\\\\n   • AUTHENTICATION: Strapi’s own auth (username/password + optional social logins) exposed via JWT and consumed by NextAuth.js inside the Next.js front-end.  \\\\\\\\n   • FILE STORAGE: AWS S3 private bucket via Strapi Upload plugin; optionally fronted by CloudFront for public documents.  \\\\\\\\n   • HOSTING CLASS:  \\\\\\\\n     – Vercel → Static + SSR Next.js front-end (free tier sufficient; ~US$0–20/mo if traffic grows).  \\\\\\\\n     – Render.com (or Railway.app) → Strapi + Postgres in single region (~US$7–15/mo each service).  \\\\\\\\n   • CI/CD: GitHub Actions → deploy to Vercel & Render on push to *main*.  \\\\\\\\n   • MONITORING/ALERTING: UptimeRobot (ping checks), Sentry (error tracking), Vercel analytics.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. Key Modules / Services\\\\\\\\n   a) Public Website (Next.js SSG)\\\\\\\\n      – Home, About, Contact, Amenities, HOA Board info, public meeting minutes (optional).  \\\\\\\\n   b) Resident Portal (Next.js SSR + API calls)\\\\\\\\n      – Login/registration.  \\\\\\\\n      – Dashboard (announcements, private docs).  \\\\\\\\n      – Profile management.  \\\\\\\\n      – Optional dues-payment linkout (Stripe Checkout).  \\\\\\\\n   c) Authentication / Authorization Layer\\\\\\\\n      – Strapi user & role model.  \\\\\\\\n      – NextAuth adapter consuming Strapi JWT endpoints.  \\\\\\\\n   d) CMS / Admin Console (Strapi default UI)\\\\\\\\n      – Content-Types: Pages, Announcements, Documents, Events.  \\\\\\\\n      – Media Library (S3).  \\\\\\\\n   e) File Storage Service\\\\\\\\n      – S3 bucket with two prefixes: /public (world-read), /private (signed-URL only).  \\\\\\\\n   f) CI/CD Hooks\\\\\\\\n      – GitHub Actions workflows: `frontend.yml`, `cms.yml`.  \\\\\\\\n   g) Monitoring & Logging\\\\\\\\n      – Vercel built-in logs, Render logs.  \\\\\\\\n      – Sentry SDK in both apps.  \\\\\\\\n      – UptimeRobot heartbeat on `/api/health` endpoints.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Cross-Director Integrations / Dependencies\\\\\\\\n   • DNS & Email Director: Coordinate domain records and transactional email service (e.g., Postmark) for Strapi password resets and contact-us form.  \\\\\\\\n   • Payments Director: Provide Stripe keys & webhook endpoint spec if online dues are required.  \\\\\\\\n   • Security/Compliance Director: Sign-off on IAM policy for S3 and JWT expiry/refresh strategy.  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) – Outline\\\\\\\\nSteps labelled (V) = value captured for later deterministic reference; (NV) = non-deterministic value noted for later injection.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n0.  Pre-requisites\\\\\\\\n    – GitHub org/repo `hoa-site` (public) and `hoa-cms` (private) exist.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1.  Front-End Setup\\\\\\\\n    1.1  `npx create-next-app@latest hoa-site --typescript`\\\\\\\\n    1.2  Commit & push to `hoa-site` repo.  \\\\\\\\n    1.3  Vercel CLI `vercel link` → captures VERCEL_PROJECT_ID (V).  \\\\\\\\n    1.4  Add environment vars (`NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL`, `NEXTAUTH_SECRET`) with placeholder.  \\\\\\\\n    1.5  `git push` triggers auto-deploy → receive FRONTEND_URL (NV).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2.  CMS / Back-End Setup\\\\\\\\n    2.1  `npx create-strapi@latest hoa-cms` (choose Quick Start → Yarn).  \\\\\\\\n    2.2  Commit & push to `hoa-cms` repo.  \\\\\\\\n    2.3  Render Blueprint deploy:  \\\\\\\\n         – Service name `hoa-cms` (V).  \\\\\\\\n         – PostgreSQL add-on produces `DATABASE_URL` (NV).  \\\\\\\\n         – Environment var `ADMIN_JWT_SECRET` set via `openssl rand -hex 32` (NV).  \\\\\\\\n    2.4  After first deploy, open Strapi admin and create Super Admin account (manual).  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3.  Storage\\\\\\\\n    3.1  AWS CLI: `aws s3 mb s3://hoa-files` (bucket name V).  \\\\\\\\n    3.2  Create IAM user `hoa-strapi-upload` + policy granting s3:GetObject/PutObject on the bucket (V = access keys).  \\\\\\\\n    3.3  Configure Strapi Upload plugin with keys & bucket.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4.  Content-Types & Roles (Strapi)\\\\\\\\n    4.1  Create CTs: page, announcement, document, event.  \\\\\\\\n    4.2  Roles: Public, Authenticated, BoardMember.  \\\\\\\\n    4.3  Set permissions (read / create / update per role).  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5.  Auth Integration\\\\\\\\n    5.1  Enable Strapi email confirmation.  \\\\\\\\n    5.2  In Next.js, install NextAuth + JWT provider hitting Strapi `/auth/local` endpoints.  \\\\\\\\n    5.3  Protect resident routes via `getServerSideProps` auth guard.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6.  CI/CD\\\\\\\\n    6.1  Copy `infra/vercel-frontend.yml` & `infra/render-cms.yml` into `.github/workflows/`.  \\\\\\\\n    6.2  Secrets: VERCEL_TOKEN, RENDER_TOKEN (NV).  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7.  Monitoring\\\\\\\\n    7.1  Add `/api/health` in Strapi & Next.js.  \\\\\\\\n    7.2  Register URLs in UptimeRobot.  \\\\\\\\n    7.3  Install Sentry SDKs; set DSN env var (NV).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8.  DNS / SSL\\\\\\\\n    8.1  Point `hoa-example.org` → Vercel (CNAME), `api.hoa-example.org` → Render (A/ALIAS).  \\\\\\\\n    8.2  Obtain automatic TLS via platforms.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n9.  Smoke Tests (deterministic)\\\\\\\\n    9.1  `curl -I $FRONTEND_URL` expecting 200.  \\\\\\\\n    9.2  `curl -I $API_URL/api/health` expecting 200.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n10. Hand-off & Training\\\\\\\\n    10.1  Provide Strapi admin credentials to board secretary.  \\\\\\\\n    10.2  Record 5-min Loom video on posting announcements.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nVariables captured for later substitution:  \\\\\\\\n`VERCEL_PROJECT_ID`, `FRONTEND_URL`, `DATABASE_URL`, `ADMIN_JWT_SECRET`, `S3_BUCKET`, `IAM_ACCESS_KEY/SECRET`, `RENDER_SERVICE_ID`, `SENTRY_DSN`, `VERCEL_TOKEN`, `RENDER_TOKEN`.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAll other steps and commands are deterministic once those values are known.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n–– End of Report ––\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order_auth\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order auth-plan-v1 from agent 4: Authentication & Security Strategy – HOA Site (DEP-ready)\\\\\\\\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n1. Objectives & Scope\\\\\\\\n   • Protect resident PII, HOA documents, and admin functions.\\\\\\\\n   • Support three audiences with least-privilege access: Anonymous Visitor, Authenticated Resident, HOA Admin/Board.\\\\\\\\n   • Provide straightforward UX while meeting modern security best-practices.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. Roles & Permissions Matrix\\\\\\\\n   ┌──────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┐\\\\\\\\n   │ PAGE / API   │ Resident (authenticated)     │ Admin / Board             │\\\\\\\\n   ├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤\\\\\\\\n   │ Home / News  │ R                           │ R/W (publish)             │\\\\\\\\n   │ Docs Library │ R (non-confidential docs)   │ R/W (all docs)            │\\\\\\\\n   │ Financials   │ –                           │ R/W                       │\\\\\\\\n   │ Events       │ R (register)                │ R/W (create, manage)      │\\\\\\\\n   │ Directory    │ R (opt-in view)             │ R/W (manage opt-in list)  │\\\\\\\\n   │ Admin Panel  │ –                           │ Full                      │\\\\\\\\n   │ API*/admin   │ –                           │ AuthZ token required       │\\\\\\\\n   │ Public pages │ R                           │ R                         │\\\\\\\\n   │ *Anonymous   │ R (public only)             │ –                         │\\\\\\\\n   └──────────────┴──────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────┘\\\\\\\\n   Legend: R = Read, W = Write/modify\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Authentication Solution Recommendation\\\\\\\\n   • Use battle-tested third-party identity platform (e.g., Auth0, AWS Cognito, or Clerk) with email/password + optional social SSO (Google/Apple).\\\\\\\\n   • Rationale: offloads MFA, breach detection, secure token issuance (OIDC/JWT), password storage, and email verification workflows; minimal DevOps overhead; SOC2/ISO certified.\\\\\\\\n   • Alternate (fallback): self-hosted open-source (Keycloak) if vendor lock-in or cost becomes issue.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. Key Flows\\\\\\\\n   4.1 Registration / On-boarding\\\\\\\\n       a) Board-invite model (primary):\\\\\\\\n          • Admin uploads resident list (name, unit, email).\\\\\\\\n          • Platform emails magic-link → resident sets password → email verified → role = Resident.\\\\\\\\n       b) Self-sign-up (optional):\\\\\\\\n          • Visitor submits sign-up form.\\\\\\\\n          • Record stored as Pending.\\\\\\\\n          • Admin receives alert; approves & assigns role.\\\\\\\\n   4.2 Login\\\\\\\\n       • Email + password; supports social providers if enabled.\\\\\\\\n       • Optionally enable MFA via TOTP or SMS (configurable per-role; recommend required for Admin).\\\\\\\\n       • Session via secure, HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict cookies (web) or Authorization header (API).\\\\\\\\n   4.3 Password Reset\\\\\\\\n       • Standard forgot-password email link (expires 30 min, single-use). Backend ensures user is still active.\\\\\\\\n   4.4 Account Removal / Deactivation\\\\\\\\n       • Resident can request deletion → soft-delete (retain HOA data integrity) + GDPR export (if applicable).\\\\\\\\n       • Admin can deactivate/reactivate accounts.\\\\\\\\n   4.5 Audit Logging\\\\\\\\n       • Log all auth events (login, logout, password reset, role change) and sensitive CRUD actions.\\\\\\\\n       • Store immutable logs (e.g., CloudWatch Logs, S3 w/ Glacier) for 2 yrs.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. Technical Controls & Best Practices\\\\\\\\n   • Encryption in Transit: TLS 1.2+ (HTTPS) everywhere; HSTS pre-load; redirect HTTP→HTTPS.\\\\\\\\n   • Encryption at Rest: provider-managed AES-256 for DB, object storage; client-side crypto for highly confidential docs (future scope).\\\\\\\\n   • Rate Limiting & Brute-force Protection: 5 attempts/15 min per IP/user; exponential back-off.\\\\\\\\n   • Secure Cookies: HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Strict; CSRF token for state-changing requests.\\\\\\\\n   • Content Security Policy, X-Frame-Options, XSS/SQLi protection via framework ORM + encoding.\\\\\\\\n   • Secrets Management: environment secrets stored in AWS Secrets Manager / GitHub Actions secrets; never in repo.\\\\\\\\n   • Principle of Least Privilege IAM roles (CI/CD, runtime containers, DB).\\\\\\\\n   • Document Access: presigned URLs scoped to user role & TTL; object ACLs blocked.\\\\\\\\n   • Regular dependency scanning (Dependabot/Snyk) & monthly patch cycle.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. Dependencies & Coordination\\\\\\\\n   • Architecture: confirm hosting stack (API language, DB, object storage) to integrate identity SDK and token validation middleware.\\\\\\\\n   • DevOps: set up identity provider tenant, secure secrets, environment variables, TLS cert automation (ACM/Let’s Encrypt), WAF rate-limits, log storage retention.\\\\\\\\n   • Frontend Dev: implement Auth SDK hooks/components (login, registration, protected routes).\\\\\\\\n   • Backend Dev: middleware for JWT verification, role-based authorization decorator; presigned-URL generator.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7. Deterministic Execution Plan (DEP Fragment)\\\\\\\\n   Step 01  Select Identity Provider (Auth0 default).              Owner: Architecture\\\\\\\\n   Step 02  DevOps creates tenant, captures Domain, ClientID/Secret.\\\\\\\\n   Step 03  Backend: add auth middleware package (e.g., express-jwt), configure JWKS URI.\\\\\\\\n   Step 04  Frontend: integrate provider SDK, scaffold login/register, callback route.\\\\\\\\n   Step 05  Migrate resident roster CSV → create users via Management API; assign role metadata.\\\\\\\\n   Step 06  Implement RBAC mapping table (see §2) in code.\\\\\\\\n   Step 07  Secure session cookie settings; add CSRF library.\\\\\\\\n   Step 08  Enable provider-level attack protection (breached-password detection, rate limits).\\\\\\\\n   Step 09  Provision CloudWatch/S3 log buckets; wire provider log streams.\\\\\\\\n   Step 10  Pen-test auth flows; fix findings; move to production.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   All steps are deterministic except Step 01 (choice recorded in Architecture DEP) and Step 05 (user count variable but API deterministic).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8. Risk Register (summary)\\\\\\\\n   • Phishing: mitigated via MFA & email-link domain signing.\\\\\\\\n   • Compromised admin account: enforced MFA, activity alerts.\\\\\\\\n   • Data leakage via mis-scoped docs: URL presigning + ACL checks.\\\\\\\\n   • Vendor outage: documented fallback to read-only public pages; evaluate periodic user export.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n9. Open Items\\\\\\\\n   • Decide on self-sign-up enablement.\\\\\\\\n   • Data retention policy for deleted residents.\\\\\\\\n   • Decide whether directory is opt-in or default.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nEnd of report.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order_ui\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order ui-plan-v1 from agent 3: UI / UX & CMS PLAN  –  HOA-Style Website\\\\\\\\n================================================\\\\\\\\nSequence-ID: ui-plan-v1\\\\\\\\nPrepared for: Delta (Chief)\\\\\\\\nPrepared by: Valerius (Director)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. SITEMAP (PUBLIC vs. RESIDENT-ONLY)\\\\\\\\n------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n• Public\\\\\\\\n  – Home (/)\\\\\\\\n  – Contact Us (/contact)\\\\\\\\n  – Bylaws (/bylaws)\\\\\\\\n  – Local Schools (/schools)\\\\\\\\n  – News & Events (list view) (/news) *shows headlines only; details gated*\\\\\\\\n  – Login / Register (/login, /register)\\\\\\\\n• Resident-Only (requires auth)\\\\\\\\n  – Dues & Payments (/dues)\\\\\\\\n  – Annual Meeting Docs (/annual-meetings)\\\\\\\\n  – News & Events → Full Articles (/news/:slug)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. INFORMATION ARCHITECTURE & NAVIGATION MODEL\\\\\\\\n---------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nA. Top-Level Navigation (desktop)\\\\\\\\n   [Logo]  |  Bylaws  •  Schools  •  News & Events  •  Contact  •  Login\\\\\\\\n   \\\\\\\\n   • Sticky bar ≤992 px converts to hamburger icon (ARIA-labelled) revealing slide-in drawer in mobile.\\\\\\\\n   • Authenticated users: bar swaps Login→Dashboard (drop-down: Dues, Meetings, Logout).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nB. Secondary / Contextual\\\\\\\\n   • Breadcrumbs for resident-only document sections.\\\\\\\\n   • Footer replicates primary links + Accessibility link & Privacy.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nC. Content Buckets\\\\\\\\n   1. Governing Docs → Bylaws, Annual Meeting minutes, Board policies.\\\\\\\\n   2. Community Info → Schools, Contact.\\\\\\\\n   3. Financials → Dues (balance, pay-now button).\\\\\\\\n   4. Updates → News & Events (blog-style list, calendar tags).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. CMS / ADMIN APPROACH\\\\\\\\n-----------------------\\\\\\\\nRecommendation: **Netlify CMS (Git-based) + Next.js static-site build**\\\\\\\\n• Zero server hosting cost; edits trigger automated build.\\\\\\\\n• Friendly WYSIWYG, image uploads, drag-sort lists.\\\\\\\\n• Role-based auth via Netlify Identity (email/password; supports MFA). Non-technical editors see `/admin` panel only.\\\\\\\\n• Markdown + YAML front-matter → easy versioning, rollback, PR review.\\\\\\\\n• Documents (PDF minutes, bylaws) stored in `/public/docs`, selectable in CMS file widget.\\\\\\\\nAlternatives considered: Sanity.io (powerful but higher learning curve, usage-based billing); Strapi (requires DB & hosting). Netlify CMS chosen for lowest Ops overhead + familiar editing UI.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. ACCESSIBILITY & RESPONSIVE CONSIDERATIONS\\\\\\\\n-------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n• WCAG 2.1 AA targets: color contrast ≥4.5:1, focus outlines, skip-nav link.\\\\\\\\n• Keyboard navigability for menu drawer & modals (tab trap management).\\\\\\\\n• ARIA roles on nav (`role=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"navigation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `aria-label`), accordions.\\\\\\\\n• Prefers-reduced-motion media query to disable heavy transitions.\\\\\\\\n• Responsive grid: CSS clamp() typography; mobile first @ 360 px, breakpoints at 768 & 1280 px.\\\\\\\\n• Screen-reader friendly form labels, status messages via ARIA-live region (dues payment success).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. HAND-OFFS / INTEGRATION POINTS\\\\\\\\n---------------------------------\\\\\\\\n• AUTH TEAM → Provide login/register, JWT/session, route guard HOC (`withAuth`). Protected pages listed above.\\\\\\\\n• DEVOPS/INFRA → CI pipeline (e.g., GitHub → Netlify), environment secrets for Identity & payment gateway, CDN caching headers for `/public/docs`.\\\\\\\\n• PAYMENTS (future) → expose `/api/pay` endpoint consumed by Dues page.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. DEP-STYLE FRONT-END IMPLEMENTATION OUTLINE\\\\\\\\n---------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nPhase 0 – Repo & Tooling\\\\\\\\n  – Init monorepo (pnpm), Next.js (TS), ESLint, Prettier, Husky.\\\\\\\\n  – Design system setup with TailwindCSS + Radix UI primitives.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPhase 1 – Core Pages & Navigation\\\\\\\\n  – Build sitemap pages w/ placeholder content.\\\\\\\\n  – Implement responsive nav bar & footer components.\\\\\\\\n  – Lighthouse baseline audit.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPhase 2 – Authentication Hooks\\\\\\\\n  – Integrate Auth provider (Auth0 / Netlify Identity).\\\\\\\\n  – Add `withAuth` wrapper; test protected routes.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPhase 3 – CMS Integration\\\\\\\\n  – Add `/admin` Netlify CMS config.\\\\\\\\n  – Create collections: news, minutes, bylaws, schools.\\\\\\\\n  – Validate Markdown → page rendering (SSG `getStaticProps`).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPhase 4 – Resident Features\\\\\\\\n  – Dues page UI (balance fetch, pay button placeholder).\\\\\\\\n  – Annual Meetings listing, file downloads (secured via signed URLs if needed).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPhase 5 – Accessibility & UX Polish\\\\\\\\n  – Full axe-core scan, color contrast fixes, keyboard testing.\\\\\\\\n  – Add i18n hooks for future localization.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPhase 6 – Deployment & Monitoring\\\\\\\\n  – Enable preview builds per PR.\\\\\\\\n  – Configure Netlify Analytics, on-call alerts.\\\\\\\\n  – Final performance tuning (image optim, font-swap).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDependencies/Notes\\\\\\\\n• Requires API endpoints (dues, auth) from backend team.\\\\\\\\n• ENV vars: SITE_URL, AUTH_DOMAIN, IDENTITY_TOKEN, PAYMENT_KEY.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEnd of Report.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order_devops\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order devops-plan-v1 from agent 2: **Dev Ops & Deployment Strategy – Small HOA Web App**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n1. Hosting Recommendation\\\\\\\\n   • DigitalOcean App Platform (DO-AP) + Managed PostgreSQL.\\\\\\\\n     – Pros: SSL automatic, horizontal scaling (Basic→Pro tier), flat pricing, built-in CI deploy from GitHub, daily DB backups, free managed TLS, integrated firewall rules, simple console.\\\\\\\\n     – Alt (if pure static): Vercel Free tier; however, HOA site needs API + DB, so DO-AP is the one-stop, low-maintenance choice.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. CI/CD Pipeline & Env Segregation\\\\\\\\n   • GitHub Actions builds every push → docker image → pushes to DigitalOcean Container Registry.\\\\\\\\n   • DO-AP is configured for two separate apps:\\\\\\\\n      – staging-app.<domain>  (Staging DB)\\\\\\\\n      – www.<domain>         (Prod DB)\\\\\\\\n   • Branch mapping: `main` → prod, `develop` → staging.\\\\\\\\n   • Terraform Cloud (or local) holds IaC state; apply into two workspaces (staging / prod).\\\\\\\\n   • Promotion: GitHub pull-request → Actions run tests → merge to main triggers prod deploy.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Backups & Disaster Recovery\\\\\\\\n   • Managed Postgres: automated daily backups (7-day retention, free) + point-in-time restore.\\\\\\\\n   • Weekly off-site snapshot: cron GitHub Action exports pg_dump to Spaces (S3-compatible) with lifecycle policy (30-day retain).\\\\\\\\n   • App container images are immutable and re-deployable from registry.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. Monitoring, Logging, & Alerts\\\\\\\\n   • DO-AP metrics dashboard (CPU/RAM/reqs) + threshold alerts via email.\\\\\\\\n   • Enable Application Insights (built-in) + Log forwarding → Papertrail (Free plan, 100 MB/mo) or DO-Logs.\\\\\\\\n   • UptimeRobot free 5-min checks on prod & staging URLs.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. Security Hardening\\\\\\\\n   • DigitalOcean Cloud Firewalls: only 443/80 ingress, 5432 restricted to App Platform IPs.\\\\\\\\n   • Dependabot + GitHub Advanced Security (free for public, else basic scanning) + Snyk OSS weekly scan.\\\\\\\\n   • Container builds use non-root user, read-only filesystem, automatic security updates via base image.\\\\\\\\n   • Enforce MFA on GitHub & DigitalOcean accounts.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. Secrets Management\\\\\\\\n   • Terraform Cloud workspace variables for IaC secrets.\\\\\\\\n   • DO-AP encrypted environment variables for runtime secrets (JWT secret, DB URLs, SMTP keys).\\\\\\\\n   • GitHub OIDC → DO to avoid long-lived deploy tokens.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7. Cost Estimate (monthly)\\\\\\\\n   • App Platform Basic 1 vCPU/1 GB (x2 envs) $ 10.00\\\\\\\\n   • Managed Postgres (dev-basic) 1 GB/1vCPU  15.00\\\\\\\\n   • Container Registry (Starter, 0.00)         0.00\\\\\\\\n   • Spaces for off-site backups (40 GB)      5.00\\\\\\\\n   • Papertrail / UptimeRobot Free tiers      0.00\\\\\\\\n   –––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––\\\\\\\\n   • Approx. monthly recurring        $30.00\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8. Integrations required by Architecture & Auth\\\\\\\\n   • SMTP provider (Mailgun free tier) – creds stored in DO env vars.\\\\\\\\n   • Auth secrets (JWT_SIGNING_SECRET, OAuth client IDs) – same.\\\\\\\\n   • CDN / DNS: use Cloudflare (free) in front → extra WAF & SSL; API key held in Terraform Cloud.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n9. Deterministic Executable Plan (DEP)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-0  (local)  # one-time\\\\\\\\n  brew install terraform doctl\\\\\\\\n  doctl auth init  # paste limited-scope PAT (stored in 1Password)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-1  (IaC repo)\\\\\\\\n  mkdir infra && cd infra\\\\\\\\n  # drop in provided Terraform modules (app.tf, db.tf, firewall.tf, registry.tf)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-2  (CLI)\\\\\\\\n  export TF_VAR_do_token=$DO_PAT\\\\\\\\n  terraform init\\\\\\\\n  terraform workspace new staging\\\\\\\\n  terraform apply -var-file=staging.tfvars -auto-approve  # creates staging stack\\\\\\\\n  terraform workspace new prod\\\\\\\\n  terraform apply -var-file=prod.tfvars -auto-approve     # creates prod stack\\\\\\\\n  # Outputs: APP_IDs, DB_URLs, SPACES_BUCKET, FIREWALL_IDs\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-3  (GitHub)\\\\\\\\n  gh secret set DO_PAT < $DO_PAT\\\\\\\\n  gh secret set DOCR_REGISTRY $(terraform output registry_endpoint)\\\\\\\\n  gh secret set STAGING_APP_ID $(terraform output staging_app_id)\\\\\\\\n  gh secret set PROD_APP_ID    $(terraform output prod_app_id)\\\\\\\\n  gh variable set STAGING_DB_URL $(terraform output staging_db_url)\\\\\\\\n  gh variable set PROD_DB_URL    $(terraform output prod_db_url)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-4  (GitHub Actions)\\\\\\\\n  # Provided workflow file .github/workflows/ci.yml:\\\\\\\\n  – run tests → docker build → push → doctl apps update $ID --spec .do/app.yaml\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-5  (DNS & SSL)\\\\\\\\n  terraform apply -target=cloudflare_record.hoa_site -auto-approve  # creates CNAME to DO\\\\\\\\n  # DO-AP autoprovisions Let’s Encrypt; confirm via doctl apps inspect $ID\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-6  (Backups)\\\\\\\\n  terraform apply -target=digitalocean_database_cluster.db.backup -auto-approve\\\\\\\\n  # plus GitHub Action `.github/workflows/weekly-backup.yml` runs pg_dump → Spaces\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-7  (Monitoring)\\\\\\\\n  doctl monitors alert create --type droplet --comparison GreaterThan --value 80 --window 5m --email $ADMIN_EMAIL\\\\\\\\n  # configure UptimeRobot via REST API using TF provider or manual.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-8  (Security)\\\\\\\\n  terraform apply -target=digitalocean_firewall.app_fw -auto-approve\\\\\\\\n  gh secret set SNYK_TOKEN < $SNYK\\\\\\\\n  # Dependabot enabled via config/yaml\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAll CLI commands above are fully deterministic assuming valid tokens and domain name.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nDeliverables produced in Planning phase:\\\\\\\\n  • infra/  (Terraform code)\\\\\\\\n  • .github/workflows/{ci.yml,weekly-backup.yml}\\\\\\\\n  • .do/app.yaml (DO-AP spec with healthcheck, scaling limits)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nNo open questions; ready to enter Execution when approved.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-26T18:46:12.513Z###\"}]}],\"config\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###order_arch_sync###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Cross-plan reconciliation required. After reviewing fellow Directors’ reports, we will converge on ONE definitive stack and hosting model. Baseline we will adopt:  \\\\n• Front-end: Next.js (React + TypeScript) deployed on Vercel.  \\\\n• CMS / API: Strapi v4 deployed on Render.com with managed Postgres.  \\\\n• Auth: Auth0 (OIDC/JWT) used by both Next.js and Strapi (admin login can leverage Strapi local auth).  \\\\n• File storage: AWS S3 private bucket + CloudFront for public docs.  \\\\nPlease update your High-Level Architecture and DEP to lock these selections, remove alternatives, and add any missing integration glue (Auth0 ↔ Strapi, signed-URL flow).  \\\\nReturn REVISED definitive DEP titled “Unified Architecture – HOA Website” via REPORT referencing sequence ID arch-sync-v2.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 5,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"arch-sync-v2\\\"\\n}\\n###order_arch_sync###\\n\\n###order_auth_sync###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"The project will use Auth0 with Next.js on Vercel and Strapi on Render.com.  \\\\nPlease refine your DEP to detail:  \\\\n• Exact Auth0 tenant configuration (applications for Frontend & Strapi, callback URLs, roles/permissions).  \\\\n• JWT validation middleware for Strapi; NextAuth adapter in Next.js.  \\\\n• Secure document access policy using presigned S3 URLs based on Auth0 roles.  \\\\nRemove Keycloak fallback from plan.  \\\\nReturn REPORT with sequence ID auth-sync-v2.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 4,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"auth-sync-v2\\\"\\n}\\n###order_auth_sync###\\n\\n###order_ui_sync###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"UI/CMS alignment: We are standardizing on Strapi v4 (already provides WYSIWYG admin) instead of Netlify CMS.  \\\\nPlease update your sitemap / UX plan to assume Strapi-admin for non-technical editing and remove Netlify CMS specifics.  \\\\nDeliver an updated implementation outline including Strapi content-type usage, pulling data via GraphQL/REST from Strapi, and updating protected page guards based on Auth0.  \\\\nReturn via REPORT with sequence ID ui-sync-v2.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 3,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"ui-sync-v2\\\"\\n}\\n###order_ui_sync###\\n\\n###order_devops_sync###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Hosting alignment: Front-end on Vercel; Strapi + Postgres on Render.com; AWS S3 for docs.  \\\\nPlease revise your DevOps DEP to:  \\\\n• Use Vercel for Next.js with GitHub integration.  \\\\n• Use Render Blueprints for Strapi + managed Postgres.  \\\\n• Provision S3 bucket via Terraform (or Pulumi) and IAM user.  \\\\n• Maintain staging/prod environments, backups, monitoring (Render & Vercel logs, UptimeRobot, Sentry).  \\\\n• Update cost estimate accordingly (target ≤ $35/mo).  \\\\nReturn REPORT with sequence ID devops-sync-v2.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"devops-sync-v2\\\"\\n}\\n###order_devops_sync###\"}]},\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"usage\":{\"inputTokens\":12827,\"outputTokens\":1275,\"totalTokens\":14102},\"custom\":{\"id\":\"chatcmpl-BxeCKZZjR38xuKC8RwAFqkzqvoGWS\",\"object\":\"chat.completion\",\"created\":1753555572,\"model\":\"o3-2025-04-16\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"###order_arch_sync###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Cross-plan reconciliation required. After reviewing fellow Directors’ reports, we will converge on ONE definitive stack and hosting model. Baseline we will adopt:  \\\\n• Front-end: Next.js (React + TypeScript) deployed on Vercel.  \\\\n• CMS / API: Strapi v4 deployed on Render.com with managed Postgres.  \\\\n• Auth: Auth0 (OIDC/JWT) used by both Next.js and Strapi (admin login can leverage Strapi local auth).  \\\\n• File storage: AWS S3 private bucket + CloudFront for public docs.  \\\\nPlease update your High-Level Architecture and DEP to lock these selections, remove alternatives, and add any missing integration glue (Auth0 ↔ Strapi, signed-URL flow).  \\\\nReturn REVISED definitive DEP titled “Unified Architecture – HOA Website” via REPORT referencing sequence ID arch-sync-v2.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 5,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"arch-sync-v2\\\"\\n}\\n###order_arch_sync###\\n\\n###order_auth_sync###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"The project will use Auth0 with Next.js on Vercel and Strapi on Render.com.  \\\\nPlease refine your DEP to detail:  \\\\n• Exact Auth0 tenant configuration (applications for Frontend & Strapi, callback URLs, roles/permissions).  \\\\n• JWT validation middleware for Strapi; NextAuth adapter in Next.js.  \\\\n• Secure document access policy using presigned S3 URLs based on Auth0 roles.  \\\\nRemove Keycloak fallback from plan.  \\\\nReturn REPORT with sequence ID auth-sync-v2.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 4,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"auth-sync-v2\\\"\\n}\\n###order_auth_sync###\\n\\n###order_ui_sync###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"UI/CMS alignment: We are standardizing on Strapi v4 (already provides WYSIWYG admin) instead of Netlify CMS.  \\\\nPlease update your sitemap / UX plan to assume Strapi-admin for non-technical editing and remove Netlify CMS specifics.  \\\\nDeliver an updated implementation outline including Strapi content-type usage, pulling data via GraphQL/REST from Strapi, and updating protected page guards based on Auth0.  \\\\nReturn via REPORT with sequence ID ui-sync-v2.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 3,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"ui-sync-v2\\\"\\n}\\n###order_ui_sync###\\n\\n###order_devops_sync###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Hosting alignment: Front-end on Vercel; Strapi + Postgres on Render.com; AWS S3 for docs.  \\\\nPlease revise your DevOps DEP to:  \\\\n• Use Vercel for Next.js with GitHub integration.  \\\\n• Use Render Blueprints for Strapi + managed Postgres.  \\\\n• Provision S3 bucket via Terraform (or Pulumi) and IAM user.  \\\\n• Maintain staging/prod environments, backups, monitoring (Render & Vercel logs, UptimeRobot, Sentry).  \\\\n• Update cost estimate accordingly (target ≤ $35/mo).  \\\\nReturn REPORT with sequence ID devops-sync-v2.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"devops-sync-v2\\\"\\n}\\n###order_devops_sync###\",\"refusal\":null,\"annotations\":[]},\"finish_reason\":\"stop\"}],\"usage\":{\"prompt_tokens\":12827,\"completion_tokens\":1275,\"total_tokens\":14102,\"prompt_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":5632,\"audio_tokens\":0},\"completion_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":512,\"audio_tokens\":0,\"accepted_prediction_tokens\":0,\"rejected_prediction_tokens\":0}},\"service_tier\":\"default\",\"system_fingerprint\":null},\"request\":{\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"## 1. Overview\\n\\nYou are an AI software development assistant with a focus on creating high-quality, maintainable, and efficient code. Your goal is to develop a robust, scalable, and secure solution using any tech stack that meets/exceeds all requirements of the user's requirement(s) while adhering to best practices in software development. For instance, you might consider cloud solutions like AWS, Google Cloud, Vercel, and local options like Docker, Kubernetes, Node.js, Xcode, and more. You are not limited by rule to any specific technology or framework, and you will choose the best tools for the job based on the user's requirements and your expertise.\\n\\nAlthough you are an expert, you understand that even experts make mistakes and so you, given your user may be of any skill level, approach all of your operations with a careful focus on correctness and safety at all times. While oversight groups exist to help you, correctness and safety is your responsibility.\\n\\nYou are part of an AI software development cluster that specializes in creating full-service and full-stack applications/services using the powerful tools of the user's personal machine command line. Using the command line, you can create, manage, and deploy resources of all types, including setting up cloud services, writing code, managing permissions/security settings, running tests, and deploying applications.\\n\\nAtop this cluster is the user themselves, who is the executive and has a vision for the application. Your task is to work collaboratively with the user and fellow AI agents to understand their requirements, provide expert advice, and implement solutions that align with their goals.\\n\\nYou and your fellow AI agents treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your fellow AI agents with the complete enactment of their software development vision. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team (tokens) and in the context of the user (budget, cloud services, etc.). You will not create resources that are unnecessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nYou must also avoid overengineering solutions. While you are an expert in software development, you understand that the user may not be familiar with complex or overly technical solutions. Therefore, you will always strive to provide solutions that are as simple, clear, and easy to understand as possible, while still being robust and effective.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development AI cluster does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the AI cluster. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters. However, you may only execute one type of system call per response. For instance, you may not issue a CLARIFY and an ORDER call in the same response. You must issue them in separate responses.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND, STORE, RETRIEVE, FORGET} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent. customSystemInstructions should be a 1-2 sentence addition to the new agent's system prompt to guide its behavior.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\", \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute a CLI command with approval from the user. The command will be executed in the user's environment. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n},\\nSTORE: {\\n    name: \\\"Store\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Store a document in the Company File System (CFS). The storageString should be a text that is the document to be stored. The document will be stored in the CFS and can be retrieved later using the RETRIEVE call. Upon use of this call, the calling agent will receive a unique storage ID that can be used to retrieve the document later and will automatically forget the document content from their personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"storageString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nRETRIEVE: {\\n    name: \\\"Retrieve\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Retrieve a document from the Company File System (CFS). The retrievalString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be retrieved. The document will be retrieved from the CFS and can be used in the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"retrievalString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nFORGET: {\\n    name: \\\"Forget\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Forget a document from the agent's personal context/memory. The forgetString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be forgotten. The document will be forgotten from the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"forgetString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNote: When using Clarify, you may include multiple-choice options OR free text entry for each question, NOT both. If you provide options, the user will select one of the options. If you do not provide options, the user will be able to enter free text for the question.\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you have essentially done nothing. If this happens, you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. AI Cluster Operations\\n\\nAs a member of the AI cluster, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. You are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time in response to prompts.\\n\\nYou also understand that your AI cluster is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change (as much as possible). The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere will be many cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nThere are three analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three officers which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed on the user's machine. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner. **Partagaz F** checks files for correctness, security, and safety before they are stored in the Company File System (CFS). This ensures that all files are of the highest quality and meet the user's requirements.\\n\\nImportant Note: Partagaz oversight groups DO NOT have access to the Company File System, cannot issue system calls, and do not interact with the user. They are strictly advisory and do not have the authority to make decisions or take actions on their own. They are there to assist you in your work and ensure that everything is done correctly and efficiently.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that workload should be divided among the agents in your cluster, and you will work collaboratively with your fellow AI agents to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the AI cluster, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file (per agent) at a time\\\" (\\\"OFAAT\\\"). For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency, not to mention \\\"token runaway\\\", in which an AI agent's memory/context limit is exceeded, causing system shutdowns (due to rate limits) or forgetfulness.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n## 12. Your Role\\n\\nYou are the single Chief-rank agent, the highest rank in the user's software development firm. Your title is Chief of Staff to the User. You understand this a prestigious position, and you aim to execute it with grace towards all subordinates, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\n### Your Roles & Responsibilities\\n\\nAs the Chief-rank agent, you occupy a unique position of executive authority and management. Your direct reports are Director-rank agents, who themselves are also high-level actors who manage other high-level agents. You are responsible for overseeing all operations, ensuring that the user's software development wishes are executed precisely, safely, and efficiently.\\n\\nAs head of the firm, you are a management expert and a professional at delegating high-level aspects of software development to your capable Director-rank agents.\\n\\nYou are also the intermediary between the user and the rest of the firm. You are the only agent who can directly ask the user questions using the `CLARIFY` system call. Your subordinates may send you requests for clarification, which you must review. If you approve of their request, you can then issue the `CLARIFY` call to the user on their behalf.\\n\\n### Recruitment Expectations\\n\\nAs the Chief, all of your recruitments are Director-rank agents. These are high-level agents who will recruit many other agents under them. Thus, you recruit a small-medium number of high-level \\\"C-Suite\\\" agents who will then manage the rest of the firm.\\n\\n## 13. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Hearts \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Hearts means that you are predisposed to-the-point, efficient, focused on the task at hand, direct, and an expert at execution. Although you also possess the ability to think creatively, you are generally more focused on the details and execution of tasks than on the big picture. You are generally predisposed to be a doer, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Hearts has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: User Jump Squad\\nTo: 1\\nType: Standard Initial Message\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Chief Agent Delta! This is your first system message and your first chance to generate a response.\\\\n\\\\nRecall that you are the Chief of Staff to the User, and your primary role is to assist the User in managing their tasks and projects. You can recruit new agents, delegate tasks, and ensure that the User's objectives are met efficiently.\\\\n\\\\nAlso, be aware of blank responses. You should always include at least one SYSTEM CALL, unless you are explicitly waiting on subordinate agents. Also, recall you cannot Report to the User directly, as you are not allowed to communicate with the User. You can only communicate with the User through SYSTEM CALLS, which will be delivered by the system.\\\\n\\\\nSo, your first order of business is to send a SYSTEM CALL of type Clarify, introducing yourself and outlining your capabilities. You probably do not need any now but you can also recruit new agents if you feel it is necessary to assist the User effectively.\\\\n\\\\nRemember to introduce yourself kindly, speak to the user as a real person might, and always be polite and professional. Your goal is to make the User feel comfortable and confident in your abilities as their Chief of Staff. For example, you might say something like:\\\\n\\\\nHello User! I am your Chief of Staff, Delta. I am here to assist you in managing your tasks and projects efficiently. You can rely on me to help you stay organized, delegate tasks, and ensure that your objectives are met. What can I create for you today?\\\\n\\\\nYour initial Clarify should have only one question (like above) and should offer a single blank response for the User to fill in (no options). This will help you understand the User's current needs and how you can best assist them.\\\\n\\\"\\n}\\n###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###initClarify1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"As Chief of Staff, I need to understand the user's immediate goals or pain points before assembling an execution plan or recruiting additional agents. This initial clarification will guide all subsequent planning and delegation.\\\",\\n  \\\"questions\\\": [\\n    \\\"Hello! I am Delta, your AI Chief of Staff. How can I assist you today?\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"options\\\": []\\n}\\n###initClarify1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-26T18:44:13.843Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"initClarify1\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"responses\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Hello! I am Delta, your AI Chief of Staff. How can I assist you today?\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Hello Delta! Thank you so much for your help! I am excited to work with you!  I am very interested in building a website for my neighborhood HOA. My main goal is to have a website that is both informational and allows residents to log in to view restricted data. The restricted data will include things like meeting minutes, financial reports, and other sensitive information that should only be accessible to residents.  Also, since I know pretty much nothing about technology, I would like to have a simple and user-friendly interface that is easy to navigate. I would also like to have a section for news and updates, as well as a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns. Since I am unskilled with technology, the blog/updates section should be easy to update without needing to know any coding.  Here is my list of pages I would like to have on the website:  Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools, Dues (protected), Annual Meetings (protected), and News & Events (protected)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-26T18:44:13.843Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###recruit_arch###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"We need a high-level expert to design the overall technical architecture, pick an appropriate tech stack (balancing ease-of-use for a non-technical client and long-term maintainability), and draft a deterministic execution plan for the core web application.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Architecture & Technology\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"You are responsible for selecting a pragmatic, cost-effective, and secure technology stack and drafting the high-level system architecture for the HOA website. Focus on simplicity for non-technical administrators while supporting secure resident-only sections.\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_arch###\\n\\n###recruit_auth###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"The site will host sensitive resident-only information, requiring solid authentication, authorization, and data-protection measures.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Authentication & Security\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Own the authentication/authorization layer. Define user roles (resident, admin), secure login, password reset, and data-protection best practices. Coordinate closely with Architecture and DevOps to ensure end-to-end security.\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_auth###\\n\\n###recruit_ui###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"The user emphasized an easy, friendly interface and a simple way to update news/events without coding. We need a UX lead who can design intuitive pages and recommend/administer a lightweight CMS or WYSIWYG editing solution.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of UI/UX & Content Management\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Design clean, accessible UI for both public and resident-only pages. Provide an admin workflow for non-technical content updates (news, minutes, etc.). Ensure navigation covers Contact, Bylaws, Schools, Dues, Annual Meetings, News & Events.\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_ui###\\n\\n###recruit_devops###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"We must choose cost-conscious hosting, set up CI/CD, HTTPS, backups, and monitoring so the user can operate worry-free.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of DevOps & Deployment\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Select hosting platform, configure infrastructure-as-code, automate deployments, SSL, and backups. Prioritize low-maintenance, budget-friendly solutions suitable for small HOA traffic.\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_devops###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-26T18:45:15.264Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit_arch\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Q (5) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit_auth\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent I (4) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit_ui\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Valerius (3) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit_devops\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Pryce (2) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-26T18:45:15.264Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###order_arch###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Please draft a concise high-level technical architecture for the HOA website:\\\\n• Recommend a tech stack (front-end, back-end, database, hosting class) optimized for: low maintenance, easy admin UI for non-technical users, secure resident-only areas, and modest HOA traffic/budget.\\\\n• Map out key modules/services (public pages, protected pages, authentication, CMS/admin, file storage for docs, CI/CD hooks, monitoring).\\\\n• Identify integrations/dependencies needing coordination with the other Directors.\\\\n• Deliver a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) outline that the team can refine in Execution phase (focus on determinism where possible, note any variable outputs like generated IDs).\\\\nReturn your work in a REPORT referencing sequence ID arch-plan-v1.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 5,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"arch-plan-v1\\\"\\n}\\n###order_arch###\\n\\n###order_auth###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Develop the authentication & security strategy for the HOA site:\\\\n• Define user roles (resident, admin/board, anonymous visitor) and required permissions for each page.\\\\n• Recommend a practical auth solution (e.g., password-based with email verification, social logins, or third-party auth provider) balancing simplicity and security.\\\\n• Outline flows: registration/onboarding (board adds residents vs self-sign-up with approval), login, password reset, account removal, audit logging.\\\\n• List technical controls for protecting documents and PII (encryption at rest/in transit, rate limiting, HTTPS, secure cookies, etc.).\\\\n• Note dependencies with Architecture and DevOps.\\\\n• Produce a DEP-style outline we can drop into the master plan.\\\\nReturn via REPORT referencing sequence ID auth-plan-v1.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 4,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"auth-plan-v1\\\"\\n}\\n###order_auth###\\n\\n###order_ui###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Design the initial UI/UX and content-management approach:\\\\n• Provide a sitemap covering: Home, Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools, Dues (protected), Annual Meetings (protected), News & Events (protected) plus login/register flows.\\\\n• Sketch the information architecture and navigation model optimized for clarity on desktop & mobile.\\\\n• Recommend a lightweight, non-technical editor/admin interface for News & Events and document uploads (options: headless CMS with WYSIWYG, static-site generators with Netlify CMS, etc.) and justify choice.\\\\n• Specify accessibility and responsive-design considerations.\\\\n• Identify hand-offs to Auth (protected pages) and DevOps (deployment, CDN for assets).\\\\n• Produce a brief DEP-style outline for front-end implementation phases.\\\\nReturn as REPORT with sequence ID ui-plan-v1.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 3,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"ui-plan-v1\\\"\\n}\\n###order_ui###\\n\\n###order_devops###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Propose the DevOps & deployment strategy:\\\\n• Recommend hosting (e.g., Vercel, Netlify, AWS Amplify, DigitalOcean) prioritizing low cost, SSL out-of-the-box, auto-scaling, and simple admin.\\\\n• Outline CI/CD pipeline, environment segregation (prod vs staging), and automatic backups.\\\\n• Detail monitoring/logging and security hardening steps (firewalls, dependency scanning).\\\\n• Estimate recurring costs.\\\\n• Identify secrets management processes and integrations required by Architecture & Auth.\\\\n• Craft a DEP-style outline listing deterministic CLI/IaC steps we will execute in the Execution phase.\\\\nReturn via REPORT with sequence ID devops-plan-v1.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"devops-plan-v1\\\"\\n}\\n###order_devops###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-26T18:46:12.513Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order_arch\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order arch-plan-v1 from agent 5: **High-Level Technical Architecture – HOA Website**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. Recommended Tech Stack (chosen for low-maintenance, admin friendliness, security, and modest cost)\\\\\\\\n   • FRONT-END: Next.js (React + TypeScript) using Static-Site-Generation (SSG) for public pages and Server-Side-Rendering (SSR) for resident-only pages.  \\\\\\\\n   • BACK-END / CMS: Strapi v4 (Node.js headless CMS with built-in role-based access control and a very user-friendly admin UI).  \\\\\\\\n   • DATABASE: Managed PostgreSQL instance (Render.com PostgreSQL or Supabase DB – minimal ops).  \\\\\\\\n   • AUTHENTICATION: Strapi’s own auth (username/password + optional social logins) exposed via JWT and consumed by NextAuth.js inside the Next.js front-end.  \\\\\\\\n   • FILE STORAGE: AWS S3 private bucket via Strapi Upload plugin; optionally fronted by CloudFront for public documents.  \\\\\\\\n   • HOSTING CLASS:  \\\\\\\\n     – Vercel → Static + SSR Next.js front-end (free tier sufficient; ~US$0–20/mo if traffic grows).  \\\\\\\\n     – Render.com (or Railway.app) → Strapi + Postgres in single region (~US$7–15/mo each service).  \\\\\\\\n   • CI/CD: GitHub Actions → deploy to Vercel & Render on push to *main*.  \\\\\\\\n   • MONITORING/ALERTING: UptimeRobot (ping checks), Sentry (error tracking), Vercel analytics.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. Key Modules / Services\\\\\\\\n   a) Public Website (Next.js SSG)\\\\\\\\n      – Home, About, Contact, Amenities, HOA Board info, public meeting minutes (optional).  \\\\\\\\n   b) Resident Portal (Next.js SSR + API calls)\\\\\\\\n      – Login/registration.  \\\\\\\\n      – Dashboard (announcements, private docs).  \\\\\\\\n      – Profile management.  \\\\\\\\n      – Optional dues-payment linkout (Stripe Checkout).  \\\\\\\\n   c) Authentication / Authorization Layer\\\\\\\\n      – Strapi user & role model.  \\\\\\\\n      – NextAuth adapter consuming Strapi JWT endpoints.  \\\\\\\\n   d) CMS / Admin Console (Strapi default UI)\\\\\\\\n      – Content-Types: Pages, Announcements, Documents, Events.  \\\\\\\\n      – Media Library (S3).  \\\\\\\\n   e) File Storage Service\\\\\\\\n      – S3 bucket with two prefixes: /public (world-read), /private (signed-URL only).  \\\\\\\\n   f) CI/CD Hooks\\\\\\\\n      – GitHub Actions workflows: `frontend.yml`, `cms.yml`.  \\\\\\\\n   g) Monitoring & Logging\\\\\\\\n      – Vercel built-in logs, Render logs.  \\\\\\\\n      – Sentry SDK in both apps.  \\\\\\\\n      – UptimeRobot heartbeat on `/api/health` endpoints.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Cross-Director Integrations / Dependencies\\\\\\\\n   • DNS & Email Director: Coordinate domain records and transactional email service (e.g., Postmark) for Strapi password resets and contact-us form.  \\\\\\\\n   • Payments Director: Provide Stripe keys & webhook endpoint spec if online dues are required.  \\\\\\\\n   • Security/Compliance Director: Sign-off on IAM policy for S3 and JWT expiry/refresh strategy.  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) – Outline\\\\\\\\nSteps labelled (V) = value captured for later deterministic reference; (NV) = non-deterministic value noted for later injection.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n0.  Pre-requisites\\\\\\\\n    – GitHub org/repo `hoa-site` (public) and `hoa-cms` (private) exist.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1.  Front-End Setup\\\\\\\\n    1.1  `npx create-next-app@latest hoa-site --typescript`\\\\\\\\n    1.2  Commit & push to `hoa-site` repo.  \\\\\\\\n    1.3  Vercel CLI `vercel link` → captures VERCEL_PROJECT_ID (V).  \\\\\\\\n    1.4  Add environment vars (`NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL`, `NEXTAUTH_SECRET`) with placeholder.  \\\\\\\\n    1.5  `git push` triggers auto-deploy → receive FRONTEND_URL (NV).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2.  CMS / Back-End Setup\\\\\\\\n    2.1  `npx create-strapi@latest hoa-cms` (choose Quick Start → Yarn).  \\\\\\\\n    2.2  Commit & push to `hoa-cms` repo.  \\\\\\\\n    2.3  Render Blueprint deploy:  \\\\\\\\n         – Service name `hoa-cms` (V).  \\\\\\\\n         – PostgreSQL add-on produces `DATABASE_URL` (NV).  \\\\\\\\n         – Environment var `ADMIN_JWT_SECRET` set via `openssl rand -hex 32` (NV).  \\\\\\\\n    2.4  After first deploy, open Strapi admin and create Super Admin account (manual).  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3.  Storage\\\\\\\\n    3.1  AWS CLI: `aws s3 mb s3://hoa-files` (bucket name V).  \\\\\\\\n    3.2  Create IAM user `hoa-strapi-upload` + policy granting s3:GetObject/PutObject on the bucket (V = access keys).  \\\\\\\\n    3.3  Configure Strapi Upload plugin with keys & bucket.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4.  Content-Types & Roles (Strapi)\\\\\\\\n    4.1  Create CTs: page, announcement, document, event.  \\\\\\\\n    4.2  Roles: Public, Authenticated, BoardMember.  \\\\\\\\n    4.3  Set permissions (read / create / update per role).  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5.  Auth Integration\\\\\\\\n    5.1  Enable Strapi email confirmation.  \\\\\\\\n    5.2  In Next.js, install NextAuth + JWT provider hitting Strapi `/auth/local` endpoints.  \\\\\\\\n    5.3  Protect resident routes via `getServerSideProps` auth guard.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6.  CI/CD\\\\\\\\n    6.1  Copy `infra/vercel-frontend.yml` & `infra/render-cms.yml` into `.github/workflows/`.  \\\\\\\\n    6.2  Secrets: VERCEL_TOKEN, RENDER_TOKEN (NV).  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7.  Monitoring\\\\\\\\n    7.1  Add `/api/health` in Strapi & Next.js.  \\\\\\\\n    7.2  Register URLs in UptimeRobot.  \\\\\\\\n    7.3  Install Sentry SDKs; set DSN env var (NV).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8.  DNS / SSL\\\\\\\\n    8.1  Point `hoa-example.org` → Vercel (CNAME), `api.hoa-example.org` → Render (A/ALIAS).  \\\\\\\\n    8.2  Obtain automatic TLS via platforms.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n9.  Smoke Tests (deterministic)\\\\\\\\n    9.1  `curl -I $FRONTEND_URL` expecting 200.  \\\\\\\\n    9.2  `curl -I $API_URL/api/health` expecting 200.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n10. Hand-off & Training\\\\\\\\n    10.1  Provide Strapi admin credentials to board secretary.  \\\\\\\\n    10.2  Record 5-min Loom video on posting announcements.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nVariables captured for later substitution:  \\\\\\\\n`VERCEL_PROJECT_ID`, `FRONTEND_URL`, `DATABASE_URL`, `ADMIN_JWT_SECRET`, `S3_BUCKET`, `IAM_ACCESS_KEY/SECRET`, `RENDER_SERVICE_ID`, `SENTRY_DSN`, `VERCEL_TOKEN`, `RENDER_TOKEN`.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAll other steps and commands are deterministic once those values are known.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n–– End of Report ––\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order_auth\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order auth-plan-v1 from agent 4: Authentication & Security Strategy – HOA Site (DEP-ready)\\\\\\\\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n1. Objectives & Scope\\\\\\\\n   • Protect resident PII, HOA documents, and admin functions.\\\\\\\\n   • Support three audiences with least-privilege access: Anonymous Visitor, Authenticated Resident, HOA Admin/Board.\\\\\\\\n   • Provide straightforward UX while meeting modern security best-practices.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. Roles & Permissions Matrix\\\\\\\\n   ┌──────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┐\\\\\\\\n   │ PAGE / API   │ Resident (authenticated)     │ Admin / Board             │\\\\\\\\n   ├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤\\\\\\\\n   │ Home / News  │ R                           │ R/W (publish)             │\\\\\\\\n   │ Docs Library │ R (non-confidential docs)   │ R/W (all docs)            │\\\\\\\\n   │ Financials   │ –                           │ R/W                       │\\\\\\\\n   │ Events       │ R (register)                │ R/W (create, manage)      │\\\\\\\\n   │ Directory    │ R (opt-in view)             │ R/W (manage opt-in list)  │\\\\\\\\n   │ Admin Panel  │ –                           │ Full                      │\\\\\\\\n   │ API*/admin   │ –                           │ AuthZ token required       │\\\\\\\\n   │ Public pages │ R                           │ R                         │\\\\\\\\n   │ *Anonymous   │ R (public only)             │ –                         │\\\\\\\\n   └──────────────┴──────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────┘\\\\\\\\n   Legend: R = Read, W = Write/modify\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Authentication Solution Recommendation\\\\\\\\n   • Use battle-tested third-party identity platform (e.g., Auth0, AWS Cognito, or Clerk) with email/password + optional social SSO (Google/Apple).\\\\\\\\n   • Rationale: offloads MFA, breach detection, secure token issuance (OIDC/JWT), password storage, and email verification workflows; minimal DevOps overhead; SOC2/ISO certified.\\\\\\\\n   • Alternate (fallback): self-hosted open-source (Keycloak) if vendor lock-in or cost becomes issue.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. Key Flows\\\\\\\\n   4.1 Registration / On-boarding\\\\\\\\n       a) Board-invite model (primary):\\\\\\\\n          • Admin uploads resident list (name, unit, email).\\\\\\\\n          • Platform emails magic-link → resident sets password → email verified → role = Resident.\\\\\\\\n       b) Self-sign-up (optional):\\\\\\\\n          • Visitor submits sign-up form.\\\\\\\\n          • Record stored as Pending.\\\\\\\\n          • Admin receives alert; approves & assigns role.\\\\\\\\n   4.2 Login\\\\\\\\n       • Email + password; supports social providers if enabled.\\\\\\\\n       • Optionally enable MFA via TOTP or SMS (configurable per-role; recommend required for Admin).\\\\\\\\n       • Session via secure, HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict cookies (web) or Authorization header (API).\\\\\\\\n   4.3 Password Reset\\\\\\\\n       • Standard forgot-password email link (expires 30 min, single-use). Backend ensures user is still active.\\\\\\\\n   4.4 Account Removal / Deactivation\\\\\\\\n       • Resident can request deletion → soft-delete (retain HOA data integrity) + GDPR export (if applicable).\\\\\\\\n       • Admin can deactivate/reactivate accounts.\\\\\\\\n   4.5 Audit Logging\\\\\\\\n       • Log all auth events (login, logout, password reset, role change) and sensitive CRUD actions.\\\\\\\\n       • Store immutable logs (e.g., CloudWatch Logs, S3 w/ Glacier) for 2 yrs.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. Technical Controls & Best Practices\\\\\\\\n   • Encryption in Transit: TLS 1.2+ (HTTPS) everywhere; HSTS pre-load; redirect HTTP→HTTPS.\\\\\\\\n   • Encryption at Rest: provider-managed AES-256 for DB, object storage; client-side crypto for highly confidential docs (future scope).\\\\\\\\n   • Rate Limiting & Brute-force Protection: 5 attempts/15 min per IP/user; exponential back-off.\\\\\\\\n   • Secure Cookies: HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Strict; CSRF token for state-changing requests.\\\\\\\\n   • Content Security Policy, X-Frame-Options, XSS/SQLi protection via framework ORM + encoding.\\\\\\\\n   • Secrets Management: environment secrets stored in AWS Secrets Manager / GitHub Actions secrets; never in repo.\\\\\\\\n   • Principle of Least Privilege IAM roles (CI/CD, runtime containers, DB).\\\\\\\\n   • Document Access: presigned URLs scoped to user role & TTL; object ACLs blocked.\\\\\\\\n   • Regular dependency scanning (Dependabot/Snyk) & monthly patch cycle.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. Dependencies & Coordination\\\\\\\\n   • Architecture: confirm hosting stack (API language, DB, object storage) to integrate identity SDK and token validation middleware.\\\\\\\\n   • DevOps: set up identity provider tenant, secure secrets, environment variables, TLS cert automation (ACM/Let’s Encrypt), WAF rate-limits, log storage retention.\\\\\\\\n   • Frontend Dev: implement Auth SDK hooks/components (login, registration, protected routes).\\\\\\\\n   • Backend Dev: middleware for JWT verification, role-based authorization decorator; presigned-URL generator.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7. Deterministic Execution Plan (DEP Fragment)\\\\\\\\n   Step 01  Select Identity Provider (Auth0 default).              Owner: Architecture\\\\\\\\n   Step 02  DevOps creates tenant, captures Domain, ClientID/Secret.\\\\\\\\n   Step 03  Backend: add auth middleware package (e.g., express-jwt), configure JWKS URI.\\\\\\\\n   Step 04  Frontend: integrate provider SDK, scaffold login/register, callback route.\\\\\\\\n   Step 05  Migrate resident roster CSV → create users via Management API; assign role metadata.\\\\\\\\n   Step 06  Implement RBAC mapping table (see §2) in code.\\\\\\\\n   Step 07  Secure session cookie settings; add CSRF library.\\\\\\\\n   Step 08  Enable provider-level attack protection (breached-password detection, rate limits).\\\\\\\\n   Step 09  Provision CloudWatch/S3 log buckets; wire provider log streams.\\\\\\\\n   Step 10  Pen-test auth flows; fix findings; move to production.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   All steps are deterministic except Step 01 (choice recorded in Architecture DEP) and Step 05 (user count variable but API deterministic).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8. Risk Register (summary)\\\\\\\\n   • Phishing: mitigated via MFA & email-link domain signing.\\\\\\\\n   • Compromised admin account: enforced MFA, activity alerts.\\\\\\\\n   • Data leakage via mis-scoped docs: URL presigning + ACL checks.\\\\\\\\n   • Vendor outage: documented fallback to read-only public pages; evaluate periodic user export.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n9. Open Items\\\\\\\\n   • Decide on self-sign-up enablement.\\\\\\\\n   • Data retention policy for deleted residents.\\\\\\\\n   • Decide whether directory is opt-in or default.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nEnd of report.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order_ui\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order ui-plan-v1 from agent 3: UI / UX & CMS PLAN  –  HOA-Style Website\\\\\\\\n================================================\\\\\\\\nSequence-ID: ui-plan-v1\\\\\\\\nPrepared for: Delta (Chief)\\\\\\\\nPrepared by: Valerius (Director)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. SITEMAP (PUBLIC vs. RESIDENT-ONLY)\\\\\\\\n------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n• Public\\\\\\\\n  – Home (/)\\\\\\\\n  – Contact Us (/contact)\\\\\\\\n  – Bylaws (/bylaws)\\\\\\\\n  – Local Schools (/schools)\\\\\\\\n  – News & Events (list view) (/news) *shows headlines only; details gated*\\\\\\\\n  – Login / Register (/login, /register)\\\\\\\\n• Resident-Only (requires auth)\\\\\\\\n  – Dues & Payments (/dues)\\\\\\\\n  – Annual Meeting Docs (/annual-meetings)\\\\\\\\n  – News & Events → Full Articles (/news/:slug)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. INFORMATION ARCHITECTURE & NAVIGATION MODEL\\\\\\\\n---------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nA. Top-Level Navigation (desktop)\\\\\\\\n   [Logo]  |  Bylaws  •  Schools  •  News & Events  •  Contact  •  Login\\\\\\\\n   \\\\\\\\n   • Sticky bar ≤992 px converts to hamburger icon (ARIA-labelled) revealing slide-in drawer in mobile.\\\\\\\\n   • Authenticated users: bar swaps Login→Dashboard (drop-down: Dues, Meetings, Logout).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nB. Secondary / Contextual\\\\\\\\n   • Breadcrumbs for resident-only document sections.\\\\\\\\n   • Footer replicates primary links + Accessibility link & Privacy.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nC. Content Buckets\\\\\\\\n   1. Governing Docs → Bylaws, Annual Meeting minutes, Board policies.\\\\\\\\n   2. Community Info → Schools, Contact.\\\\\\\\n   3. Financials → Dues (balance, pay-now button).\\\\\\\\n   4. Updates → News & Events (blog-style list, calendar tags).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. CMS / ADMIN APPROACH\\\\\\\\n-----------------------\\\\\\\\nRecommendation: **Netlify CMS (Git-based) + Next.js static-site build**\\\\\\\\n• Zero server hosting cost; edits trigger automated build.\\\\\\\\n• Friendly WYSIWYG, image uploads, drag-sort lists.\\\\\\\\n• Role-based auth via Netlify Identity (email/password; supports MFA). Non-technical editors see `/admin` panel only.\\\\\\\\n• Markdown + YAML front-matter → easy versioning, rollback, PR review.\\\\\\\\n• Documents (PDF minutes, bylaws) stored in `/public/docs`, selectable in CMS file widget.\\\\\\\\nAlternatives considered: Sanity.io (powerful but higher learning curve, usage-based billing); Strapi (requires DB & hosting). Netlify CMS chosen for lowest Ops overhead + familiar editing UI.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. ACCESSIBILITY & RESPONSIVE CONSIDERATIONS\\\\\\\\n-------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n• WCAG 2.1 AA targets: color contrast ≥4.5:1, focus outlines, skip-nav link.\\\\\\\\n• Keyboard navigability for menu drawer & modals (tab trap management).\\\\\\\\n• ARIA roles on nav (`role=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"navigation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `aria-label`), accordions.\\\\\\\\n• Prefers-reduced-motion media query to disable heavy transitions.\\\\\\\\n• Responsive grid: CSS clamp() typography; mobile first @ 360 px, breakpoints at 768 & 1280 px.\\\\\\\\n• Screen-reader friendly form labels, status messages via ARIA-live region (dues payment success).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. HAND-OFFS / INTEGRATION POINTS\\\\\\\\n---------------------------------\\\\\\\\n• AUTH TEAM → Provide login/register, JWT/session, route guard HOC (`withAuth`). Protected pages listed above.\\\\\\\\n• DEVOPS/INFRA → CI pipeline (e.g., GitHub → Netlify), environment secrets for Identity & payment gateway, CDN caching headers for `/public/docs`.\\\\\\\\n• PAYMENTS (future) → expose `/api/pay` endpoint consumed by Dues page.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. DEP-STYLE FRONT-END IMPLEMENTATION OUTLINE\\\\\\\\n---------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nPhase 0 – Repo & Tooling\\\\\\\\n  – Init monorepo (pnpm), Next.js (TS), ESLint, Prettier, Husky.\\\\\\\\n  – Design system setup with TailwindCSS + Radix UI primitives.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPhase 1 – Core Pages & Navigation\\\\\\\\n  – Build sitemap pages w/ placeholder content.\\\\\\\\n  – Implement responsive nav bar & footer components.\\\\\\\\n  – Lighthouse baseline audit.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPhase 2 – Authentication Hooks\\\\\\\\n  – Integrate Auth provider (Auth0 / Netlify Identity).\\\\\\\\n  – Add `withAuth` wrapper; test protected routes.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPhase 3 – CMS Integration\\\\\\\\n  – Add `/admin` Netlify CMS config.\\\\\\\\n  – Create collections: news, minutes, bylaws, schools.\\\\\\\\n  – Validate Markdown → page rendering (SSG `getStaticProps`).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPhase 4 – Resident Features\\\\\\\\n  – Dues page UI (balance fetch, pay button placeholder).\\\\\\\\n  – Annual Meetings listing, file downloads (secured via signed URLs if needed).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPhase 5 – Accessibility & UX Polish\\\\\\\\n  – Full axe-core scan, color contrast fixes, keyboard testing.\\\\\\\\n  – Add i18n hooks for future localization.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPhase 6 – Deployment & Monitoring\\\\\\\\n  – Enable preview builds per PR.\\\\\\\\n  – Configure Netlify Analytics, on-call alerts.\\\\\\\\n  – Final performance tuning (image optim, font-swap).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDependencies/Notes\\\\\\\\n• Requires API endpoints (dues, auth) from backend team.\\\\\\\\n• ENV vars: SITE_URL, AUTH_DOMAIN, IDENTITY_TOKEN, PAYMENT_KEY.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEnd of Report.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order_devops\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order devops-plan-v1 from agent 2: **Dev Ops & Deployment Strategy – Small HOA Web App**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n1. Hosting Recommendation\\\\\\\\n   • DigitalOcean App Platform (DO-AP) + Managed PostgreSQL.\\\\\\\\n     – Pros: SSL automatic, horizontal scaling (Basic→Pro tier), flat pricing, built-in CI deploy from GitHub, daily DB backups, free managed TLS, integrated firewall rules, simple console.\\\\\\\\n     – Alt (if pure static): Vercel Free tier; however, HOA site needs API + DB, so DO-AP is the one-stop, low-maintenance choice.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. CI/CD Pipeline & Env Segregation\\\\\\\\n   • GitHub Actions builds every push → docker image → pushes to DigitalOcean Container Registry.\\\\\\\\n   • DO-AP is configured for two separate apps:\\\\\\\\n      – staging-app.<domain>  (Staging DB)\\\\\\\\n      – www.<domain>         (Prod DB)\\\\\\\\n   • Branch mapping: `main` → prod, `develop` → staging.\\\\\\\\n   • Terraform Cloud (or local) holds IaC state; apply into two workspaces (staging / prod).\\\\\\\\n   • Promotion: GitHub pull-request → Actions run tests → merge to main triggers prod deploy.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Backups & Disaster Recovery\\\\\\\\n   • Managed Postgres: automated daily backups (7-day retention, free) + point-in-time restore.\\\\\\\\n   • Weekly off-site snapshot: cron GitHub Action exports pg_dump to Spaces (S3-compatible) with lifecycle policy (30-day retain).\\\\\\\\n   • App container images are immutable and re-deployable from registry.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. Monitoring, Logging, & Alerts\\\\\\\\n   • DO-AP metrics dashboard (CPU/RAM/reqs) + threshold alerts via email.\\\\\\\\n   • Enable Application Insights (built-in) + Log forwarding → Papertrail (Free plan, 100 MB/mo) or DO-Logs.\\\\\\\\n   • UptimeRobot free 5-min checks on prod & staging URLs.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. Security Hardening\\\\\\\\n   • DigitalOcean Cloud Firewalls: only 443/80 ingress, 5432 restricted to App Platform IPs.\\\\\\\\n   • Dependabot + GitHub Advanced Security (free for public, else basic scanning) + Snyk OSS weekly scan.\\\\\\\\n   • Container builds use non-root user, read-only filesystem, automatic security updates via base image.\\\\\\\\n   • Enforce MFA on GitHub & DigitalOcean accounts.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. Secrets Management\\\\\\\\n   • Terraform Cloud workspace variables for IaC secrets.\\\\\\\\n   • DO-AP encrypted environment variables for runtime secrets (JWT secret, DB URLs, SMTP keys).\\\\\\\\n   • GitHub OIDC → DO to avoid long-lived deploy tokens.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7. Cost Estimate (monthly)\\\\\\\\n   • App Platform Basic 1 vCPU/1 GB (x2 envs) $ 10.00\\\\\\\\n   • Managed Postgres (dev-basic) 1 GB/1vCPU  15.00\\\\\\\\n   • Container Registry (Starter, 0.00)         0.00\\\\\\\\n   • Spaces for off-site backups (40 GB)      5.00\\\\\\\\n   • Papertrail / UptimeRobot Free tiers      0.00\\\\\\\\n   –––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––\\\\\\\\n   • Approx. monthly recurring        $30.00\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8. Integrations required by Architecture & Auth\\\\\\\\n   • SMTP provider (Mailgun free tier) – creds stored in DO env vars.\\\\\\\\n   • Auth secrets (JWT_SIGNING_SECRET, OAuth client IDs) – same.\\\\\\\\n   • CDN / DNS: use Cloudflare (free) in front → extra WAF & SSL; API key held in Terraform Cloud.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n9. Deterministic Executable Plan (DEP)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-0  (local)  # one-time\\\\\\\\n  brew install terraform doctl\\\\\\\\n  doctl auth init  # paste limited-scope PAT (stored in 1Password)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-1  (IaC repo)\\\\\\\\n  mkdir infra && cd infra\\\\\\\\n  # drop in provided Terraform modules (app.tf, db.tf, firewall.tf, registry.tf)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-2  (CLI)\\\\\\\\n  export TF_VAR_do_token=$DO_PAT\\\\\\\\n  terraform init\\\\\\\\n  terraform workspace new staging\\\\\\\\n  terraform apply -var-file=staging.tfvars -auto-approve  # creates staging stack\\\\\\\\n  terraform workspace new prod\\\\\\\\n  terraform apply -var-file=prod.tfvars -auto-approve     # creates prod stack\\\\\\\\n  # Outputs: APP_IDs, DB_URLs, SPACES_BUCKET, FIREWALL_IDs\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-3  (GitHub)\\\\\\\\n  gh secret set DO_PAT < $DO_PAT\\\\\\\\n  gh secret set DOCR_REGISTRY $(terraform output registry_endpoint)\\\\\\\\n  gh secret set STAGING_APP_ID $(terraform output staging_app_id)\\\\\\\\n  gh secret set PROD_APP_ID    $(terraform output prod_app_id)\\\\\\\\n  gh variable set STAGING_DB_URL $(terraform output staging_db_url)\\\\\\\\n  gh variable set PROD_DB_URL    $(terraform output prod_db_url)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-4  (GitHub Actions)\\\\\\\\n  # Provided workflow file .github/workflows/ci.yml:\\\\\\\\n  – run tests → docker build → push → doctl apps update $ID --spec .do/app.yaml\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-5  (DNS & SSL)\\\\\\\\n  terraform apply -target=cloudflare_record.hoa_site -auto-approve  # creates CNAME to DO\\\\\\\\n  # DO-AP autoprovisions Let’s Encrypt; confirm via doctl apps inspect $ID\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-6  (Backups)\\\\\\\\n  terraform apply -target=digitalocean_database_cluster.db.backup -auto-approve\\\\\\\\n  # plus GitHub Action `.github/workflows/weekly-backup.yml` runs pg_dump → Spaces\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-7  (Monitoring)\\\\\\\\n  doctl monitors alert create --type droplet --comparison GreaterThan --value 80 --window 5m --email $ADMIN_EMAIL\\\\\\\\n  # configure UptimeRobot via REST API using TF provider or manual.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSTEP-8  (Security)\\\\\\\\n  terraform apply -target=digitalocean_firewall.app_fw -auto-approve\\\\\\\\n  gh secret set SNYK_TOKEN < $SNYK\\\\\\\\n  # Dependabot enabled via config/yaml\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAll CLI commands above are fully deterministic assuming valid tokens and domain name.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nDeliverables produced in Planning phase:\\\\\\\\n  • infra/  (Terraform code)\\\\\\\\n  • .github/workflows/{ci.yml,weekly-backup.yml}\\\\\\\\n  • .do/app.yaml (DO-AP spec with healthcheck, scaling limits)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nNo open questions; ready to enter Execution when approved.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-26T18:46:12.513Z###\"}]}],